{
  "description": "General-purpose hosting, paste, raw-content, IP-echo, tunnel and request-capture services. Anyone can publish on them in seconds, so a destination on these hosts says nothing about who is on the other end. The broker never offers 'always allow' for them, auto-denies them before the first user action in a tab, and the built-in baseline (and the shipped examples) must never match them. Policies may add entries with `riskyDestinations`. Matching uses policy host patterns: `*.x` is strict subdomains, so list the apex separately when it serves content too.",
  "source": "Contagious Interview (DPRK) VS Code tasks.json campaign: loader on *.vercel.app, public-IP lookup via ipify.org, fallback C2 on api.npoint.io, raw-IP C2 on port 1224. https://www.jamf.com/blog/threat-actors-expand-abuse-of-visual-studio-code/ and https://opensourcemalware.com/blog/latest-contagious-interview-malware-campaign-abuses-microsoft-vscode-tasks (both read 2026-11-04). Other entries are the same service classes.",
